hello  everyone and welcome to today's  presentation part of agilent's virtual  energy and chemical summit  i am abby pell of lab roots and i will  be your moderator for today's event  now let's get started before we begin i  would like to remind  everyone that this is interactive we  encourage you to participate by  submitting as many questions you would  as you would like during the  presentation to do so  simply click and type them into the ask  a question box  and click send we will answer as many  questions as we have  time for at the end of the presentation  if you have trouble seeing or hearing  the presentation click on the ask the  question box  located on the far left of your screen  i now present today's speaker george  gonzalez  agilent standard test program and enter  an overview addressing required testing  in energy and chemicals for a complete  biography on our speaker please visit  the biography tab at the top of your  screen  george you may now begin your  presentation  hello everyone my name is george  gonzalez  i work for agilent technologies this  presentation  is going to provide an overview  of how agilent is working with customers  through collaboration in the development  of standard test methods  at agilent our goal is to deliver  scientific  operational and economic value for your  lab  in addition to providing reliable and  robust instruments  we are collaborating with customers  towards the development of new standard  test methods  agilent is currently sponsoring numerous  standard test methods at astm and other  standard development organizations  the development and publication of a new  standard requires  approximately two to three years  i will review the process later in this  presentation  agilent is committed to collaborating  with customers  in the development of new standards we  will now review the mpaes  standards development program as an  example  we will also review a few other agilent  sponsored standards  currently in various steps in the  development  agilent introduced the microwave plasma  atomic emission spectrometer or mpaes  recently and the decision was made  to establish a standard test method  at astm  we then moved to collaborate to develop  two standards based on the mpaes  one was for the elemental analysis  of crude oil and residual fuel oil  the second proposed standard is for  the elemental analysis of biodiesel  we coordinated and established a  collaboration task group which included  12 laboratories as shown on the top  right  the global collaboration team then ran  a pilot study and a full  intra-laboratory study  over the course of two and a half to  three years  as shown on the bottom left the timeline  essentially started  with the conception of the idea back in  april of 2017  then we informally introduced the  technique  at a technical presentation at a june  meeting of astm d2  subcommittee 3 meeting and requested  some work items  for the program and move forward  the timeline follows the traditional  astm standard development program  and it flows through establishing the  task group  proceeding on to  preparing a sample set moving on to  running a pilot study determining  and scoping out the full  intra-laboratory study  based on the pilot study results and  then moving into  the promulgation stage of the  proposed standards and i will show  and preview some of the astm  procedural promulgation procedures  later in this presentation  i would like to briefly review the  organizational structure  at astm astm is formed by numerous  technical committees  and the one we have been focusing on  today is  astm d2 which is the  committee on petroleum products liquid  fuels and lubricants  this committee is formed by  four different types of subcommittees  which include the  executive subcommittee a product  subcommittees which are defined as  the letter designations for example d2d  is the committee on hydrocarbons for  chemical and special uses  and then there are property  subcommittees  which are focusing on test methods  for example d2 subcommittee 3  is for elemental analysis and then there  are  a few coordinating subcommittees now if  you notice there  within each subcommittee there are  task groups which are formed to work  on astm standards they're issued  normally by a work item which is a  focus on establishing a new standard  next i would like to review the standard  development process  at astm new standards are typically  justified  by industry need or new technology  the proposed test method is presented in  concept  to the appropriate committee if  sufficient  interest is found a task group is formed  to develop the standard  the task group then drafts the test  method and performs a pilot study  if the test method is found viable the  pilot study data  is used to develop an intra-laboratory  study  which is required to establish test  method precision  and bias upon completion the proposed  test  method is balloted with subcommittee  approval  after ballot if the ballot  passes without any negative votes the  test method moves on to main committee  balloting  however if the ballot incurs  negative votes then the concern needs to  be adjudicated  to determine a concern and whether  it's persuasive or non-persuasive if the  negative vote is found persuasive  the test method must be revised and  re-balloted at subcommittee  if the negative vote is found  non-persuasive through the  adjudication process the test method  moves on  to main committee balloting main  committee  balloting is performed the same way  negative votes are addressed using the  same procedure described above  if the ballot passes without any  negative votes  the test method moves on to the  review by committee on standards  followed by publication  the collaboration program i described  earlier  provided a new test method  astm d 8322 elemental analysis of crude  oil by mpaes  the official title is standard test  method for  determination of elements in residual  fuels  and crude oils by microwave plasma  atomic emission spectroscopy  this work as noted earlier  was completed through a collaboration  which included 12 labs  there are no other standard test methods  for the mpas in the industry  so this provides an opportunity for our  customers to  use the mpaes for commercial trade  and testing of residual fuel oil  and crude oil  standard test method d8322  provides some excellent detection range  capabilities  the valid test result range for each  type of  sample crude oil and residual fuel oil  in this case were determined through  an interlaboratory study  i think you will agree that the test  result  range shown here for each type of sample  meets or exceeds industry standards for  elemental analysis  so agilent's test method development  program included  elemental analysis of biodiesel by mpaes  this work item 56916  has progressed to  subcommittee level and is currently  being balloted  i am currently addressing a couple of  items  that were of concern to some  voting members the standard  is currently balloted and i  believe it will move on to main  committee  later this year and published in the  fall of this year  this slide provides some insight to mpas  performance  in testing biodiesel blends up to b100  again that's biodiesel blends from b1  to b100 the table shown  below contain the balloted test method  valid test result range  for elemental analysis of biodiesel  the valid test result range obtained by  performing the ils  meets or exceeds the current industry  testing performance  and i believe you will agree that the  mpaes  is an excellent instrument for testing  biodiesel blends from b1 to b100  we have also been collaborating with  customers  to revise astm d7845  it's for the determination of chemical  species  in marine fuel oil by gcms  the scope of work is to establish  precision and bias  for this standard by  running an ils in addition  we have also incorporated two new  examples of dean switch systems into  this next revision the revision has been  approved  but pending the precision and bias  information precision and bias  is being established by an ils which is  currently ongoing  the two new examples of dean's switch  have been  incorporated into the existing standard  along with minor editorial revisions  work item 50453  currently in being valid at  astm d2 committee is going to include a  revision  to include new dean switch  configurations which are available with  agilent gcs  the diagram on the left is an example of  a simplified dean switch  which vents to the splitter vent and  uses  our purged ultimate union  the diagram on the right allows for  fast back flush timing and uses  agilent's  cft these configurations will be  incorporated into the next revision of  astm  d7845 and i expect them to be  this standard to be published later in  the fall  of this year  another test method revision for d7845  is for the expansion of scope  for this standard  the expansion will include an extended  capability for additional compounds  that have been requested by iso 8217  specifications  we are currently collaborating with  intertek  which will provide the laboratory gcms  and other materials for us to work  together in the development of  what will essentially become procedure b  of d7845 in a future revision  in addition the work will require a full  intra-laboratory study  and we will require at least 10  laboratories  to establish the full precision  this work is ongoing and as noted in the  other examples  the work requires two to three years  to complete when we will see  the standard revised and published  with the expansion of scope  recently i also worked on the  development  of a test method for determination of  oxygenates in motor gasoline by gcms  in this case it would be an addition or  revision  of astm d5769  which is the test method for  determination of benzene  and total aromatics in motor gasoline  the scope of this method would  incorporate the oxygenates  into d5769  the work performed included the use of  an intubal  gcms and the robustness study  and collaboration was conducted at  bureau veritas  at the houston technical center  in pasadena texas the collaboration has  gone well  and i have presented this proposed test  method  at astm  and going forward we plan to conduct  a full laboratory interlaboratory  study  so to further expand on the scope of the  proposed test  method for determination of oxygenates  in motor gasoline by gcms  you'll see this table includes the  essentially the calibration standards  that were used to  expand the scope and establish the test  method  for this technique the range  in this case is  exactly the same as astm d5599  astm d5599 is a test method  which uses a gcofid  and is the current standard accepted  method by the us epa  in this case you see that all of the  oxygenates meet  the industry requirement for  determination of oxygenates and motor  gasoline  the difference is that we are using a  gcms  the proposed test method has been  presented at astm  and has been accepted to proceed  to revision of astm d5769  to incorporate oxygenates in motor  gasoline  this concludes my presentation on  agilent sponsored  astm standards however as i noted  we are committed to collaborating with  our customers towards the development of  new standards  as well as you might have noticed that  we are  also working on the revision of numerous  standards  so moving forward we will need  volunteers  to help us work on the development of  new standards  and revision of standards which the work  will also include  intra-laboratory studies if you are  interested in participating  please feel free to contact me if you  have any questions or comments  also feel free to contact me directly  thank you for your time today i hope you  have a great day  start the q a portion of the webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now or just click the ask a  question box located on the far left  of your screen now let's get started  the first question is is are ils  studies limited to the us  hi abby no astm  is an international organization and so  we  quite often participate uh have  laboratories participating globally  from basically all parts of the world  so the answer is no  okay all right thank you and  uh how can ha another question we have  is how do we get involved  with an ils study  well the the interested  parties can go to the astm website and  then  search for subcommittees and technical  committees  and then browse down to the standards  under  the jurisdiction of each subcommittee  you will see the standards listed as  well  at the bottom towards the end of the  list you will see  a number of work items as i mentioned in  the presentation  there's some work items that were  referenced  you can simply log in there and  send it it will send an email to the  task group a technical contact and  then you can begin the discussion that  way  thank you george um another question  uh third question here is actually from  william barber  and the question is is ox  oxena jade sorry in d5769  any expected time that this will be  approved  so as as a it's a  it's it's not an easy answer and and i  tried to  sort of elaborate uh some of that in the  process  required to establish a standard in this  case d5769 is an established standard  and and so it should proceed along  a little bit faster than uh than  normal and normal as i mentioned it may  take up to three years before  um the standard is is a new standard  is uh published so in this case uh  roughly  uh about two years and of course  i still need to present a technical  report  and request a work item and  i don't expect the work item to be  requested until  june of 2021 next year so  we're looking at about maybe three years  from now at the soonest  but uh be on the lookout i do plan to  uh publish an application note on that  determination of oxygenates and motor  gasoline in the next few months  thank you george and our last question  here  that we have um is do we have to be a  member of  astm to participate in the study  no you do not as well as the earlier  answer you can  simply contact the technical contact  under the workout item or you can  contact astm.org  and and someone will direct you to  the correct technical contact get  involved now  you still should go to find the work  item that you would like to get involved  with  and if you have a laboratory uh you know  there will be a you know a question and  answer to make sure that the laboratory  is suited  to uh participate in the study  and thank you again george for that  answer um  i want to i think that result or that is  our  questions for today if there are no more  um uh i just want to thank you  for your time today at mr gonzalez and  your important research  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us  again and questions submitted during the  on-demand period  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  uh this webcast can be viewed on demand  through  november of 2021 and until next time  thank you so much goodbye thank you abby

